'''2.''' General course or drift of a thought, saying, discourse, or the like; that course of thought or meaning which holds on or runs through a whole discourse, treatise, statute, or the like; general purport; substance.

{{smaller block|Thenne he cryed so cler that kenne myȝt alle;<br>The trwe ''tenor'' of his teme he tolde on this wyse.
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), iii. 358.}}}}

{{smaller block|Mark the ''tenor'' of my style,<br>Which shall such trembling hearts unfold<br>As seldom hath to-fore been told.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Case is Altered, i. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''tennure'' of this letter was<br>That Robbin would submit.
{{right|''True Tale of Robin Hood'' (Child's Ballads, V. 366).}}}}

{{smaller block|Emigration to the new countries was encouraged by the liberal ''tenor'' of the royal ordinances passed from time to time.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 9.}}}}

'''3.''' In ''law'': (''a'') True intent and meaning; purport and effect: as, the ''tenor'' of a deed or instrument of any kind is its purport and effect, but not its actual words. (''b'') A transcript or copy. {{smaller|It implies that a correct copy is set out, and therefore at common law, under an allegation according to the tenor, the instrument must be set out correctly.}}

'''4.''' Character; nature.

{{smaller block|All of a ''tenor'' was their after-life,<br>No day discoloured with domestic strife.
{{right|''Dryden'', Pal. and Arc., iii. 1148.}}}}

'''5.''' In ''music'': (''a'') The highest variety of the ordinary adult male voice. {{smaller|Its compass usually extends about two octaves or less from the first C below mid-}}

