2. [''cap.''] [NL. (Lacépède, 1798), and in the form ''Tanrecus'' (Desmarest, 1825).] A generic name for the species of ''Centetidæ:'' same as ''Centetes'' in a former broad sense. [Not used.]

'''tense<sup>1</sup>''' (tens), ''n.'' [Formerly also ''tence;'' < ME. ''tens, temps,'' < OF. ''tans, tens, tenzs, tems, temps,'' F. ''temps'' = Sp. ''tiempo'' = Pg. It. ''tempo,'' < L. ''tempus,'' time, in grammar tense. Cf. ''temporal<sup>1</sup>, temporary,'' etc.] 1†. Time. See ''temps.''

{{smaller block|I warne yow wel, it is to seken ever,
That future ''temps'' hath maad men to dissever
In trust therof from al that ever they hadde.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Canon's Yeoman's Tale, l. 322.}}}}

2. In ''gram.'': (''a'') Time. (''b'') One of the forms, or sets of forms, which a verb takes in order to indicate the time of action or of that which is affirmed: extended also to forms indicating the nature of the action as continued, completed, and the like. {{smaller|In English this is effected either by internal vowel change, as in ''sing, sang, lead, led;'' by terminational inflection, as in ''love, loved;'' or, in verb-phrases, by means of auxiliary words, as in ''did love, have loved, will love.''}}

{{smaller block|We may say now that we have Treasurers of all ''Tenses'', for there are four living, to wit the Lords Manchester, Middlesex, Marlborough, and the newly chosen.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. v. 2.}}}}

'''At prime tense''', at the first time; at first; instantly.

{{smaller block|My silf I knowe fulle wel Daungere,
And how he is feers of his cheere,
''At prime temps'' Love to manace.
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 3373.}}}}

