{{smaller block|'''Future, perfect, pluperfect, present tense.''' See the adjectives.—'''Historical tenses.''' See ''historical'', 4.—'''Sequence or consecution of tenses.''' See ''sequence''.}}

'''tense'''<sup>2</sup> (tens), ''a.'' [= Sp. ''tenso'', < L. ''tensus'', pp. of ''tendere'', stretch: see ''tend''<sup>1</sup>.] Being in a state of tension; stretched until tight; strained to stiffness; rigid; not lax: often used figuratively.

{{smaller block|For the free passage of the sound into the ear it is requisite that the tympanum be ''tense''.
{{right|''Holder'', Elements of Speech, p. 161.}}
Her temples were sunk, her forehead was ''tense'', and a fatal paleness sat upon her cheek.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xxviii.}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Tense abdomen,''' in ''entom.'', an abdomen neither divided into segments nor having segments indicated, as in most spiders, by transverse folds.}}

'''tense'''<sup>2</sup> (tens), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''tensed'', ppr. ''tensing''. [< ''tense''<sup>2</sup>, ''a.''] To make tense or taut. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|If, instead of a symmetrical movement, the other hand made a maximal effort of ''tensing'' the extensor instead of the flexor muscles of the hand, . . . no constant effect . . . was observed.
{{right|''Mind'', IX. 109.}}}}

'''tenseless''' (tens′les), ''a.'' [< ''tense''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less''.] Having no tense: as, a ''tenseless'' verb. ''Classical Rev.'', III. 9.

'''tenselessness''' (tens′les-nes), ''n.'' The character of being tenseless. ''Amer. Jour. Philol.'', VIII. 59.

'''tensely''' (tens′li), ''adv.'' In a tense manner; with tension.

'''tenseness''' (tens′nes), ''n.'' The state of being tense, or stretched to stiffness; stiffness; rigidness.

