2. In ''mech.'', stress, or the force by which a bar, rod, string, or the like is pulled when forming part of any system in equilibrium or in motion.

{{smaller block|In a large suspension bridge the ''tension'' produced by the occasional load is usually only a small fraction of that produced by the permanent load.
{{right|''R. S. Ball'', Exper. Mechanics, p. 232.}}}}

3. In ''physics'', a constrained condition of the particles of bodies, arising from the action of antagonistic forces, in which they tend to return to their former condition; elastic force. {{smaller|Tension may be present in a solid body, and also in a liquid in the case of surface-tension (which see), but not in a gas. What is commonly called the ''tension'' of a gas is properly its pressure simply—due, according to the kinetic theory of gases (see ''gas'', 1), to the innumerable impacts of the moving molecules against the confining surface; good writers avoid the use of ''tension'' in this sense.}}

